Reading the data
The CSV is a tidy, one-row-per-discrete-sample table. Key columns:

keyfield, cruise_id, datetime — sample identity and time;
lat_dec, lon_dec, station, station_type (C / F, as coded upstream) — location;
depth, depth_class (Surface / Mid / Bottom), cast, nisk_start, nisk_end — sampling depth and Niskin-bottle metadata;
one column per analyte (temp, sal, o2_ctd, o2_disc, nh4, po4, no3_no2, no3, no2, si, chl, pheo) plus a matching *_flag quality-control column.







Warning



Caveats. Values are reported as-is; the *_flag codebook is maintained by the upstream program and is not bundled with the CSV, so flagged values are shown but not automatically removed. A handful of physically impossible values (e.g. an o2_ctd reading in the thousands of mg/L) are also present and are handled conservatively in the plots.
